// Edit these strings when the reviewer guide content changes.

export const decisionsDocument =
  "# Banking App — Decision Record\n\nThis record explains the implemented design, its rationale, accepted trade-offs, and future improvements.\n\n## Assignment scope\n\nBuild a local banking SPA that allows a signed-in user to:\n\n- view their account balance;\n- make a simulated deposit;\n- make a simulated withdrawal; and\n- transfer money to another account.\n\nThe backend uses Node.js, TypeScript, Express, and PostgreSQL. It must authenticate users and authorize access so that a user can only view or modify their own account. Registration and transaction-history display are not required.\n\nThe repository starts empty. The implementation should remain small enough to explain confidently, with most of its sophistication concentrated in authorization and correct money movement.\n\n## Technology and local environment\n\n- React, TypeScript, and Vite for the SPA.\n- Express and TypeScript for the server.\n- Zod for request validation.\n- PostgreSQL accessed through parameterized SQL using `pg`.\n- Vitest and Supertest for testing.\n- Plain CSS with no large UI framework.\n- npm workspaces with separate `client` and `server` packages.\n- No Docker.\n- Locally installed PostgreSQL, with project scripts to create, migrate, seed, and reset the schema.\n- Database setup runs in the order `db:create`, `db:migrate`, then `db:seed`.\n- Migrations are numbered SQL files executed by a small TypeScript runner.\n- The runner records applied files in `schema_migrations` and runs each unapplied migration transactionally.\n- Migrations define structure; seed scripts insert demo data. They are intentionally separate concerns.\n- `db:create` attempts to create the configured application database. The README also provides manual fallback commands for users without database-creation permission.\n- Migration and seed scripts require no elevated privileges after the databases exist.\n- Root database commands are:\n  - `npm run db:migrate` for structure only;\n  - `npm run db:seed` for demo data only;\n  - `npm run db:reset` to rebuild the schema and then reseed it; and\n  - `npm run db:setup` to create the configured database, migrate, and seed.\n- Express does not mutate the schema automatically during startup.\n- Startup performs a lightweight database/schema check and exits with a helpful setup message when required structure is missing.\n- Proposed uncommon default ports:\n  - Vite: `47831`\n  - Express: `47832`\n  - PostgreSQL: `47833`\n- Database connection details are configurable through `.env`, with a committed `.env.example`.\n- Standard runtime and database names:\n  - Node.js 24, declared in `.nvmrc` and `package.json` engines;\n  - configured application database `banking_app`, shared by the app and database-backed tests.\n\n## Product and interface\n\n- Product name: **Banking App**.\n- Primary visual characteristics:\n  - bright cyan accent;\n  - off-white background;\n  - near-black text;\n  - pale-grey surfaces;\n  - rounded controls;\n  - spacious, restrained layouts.\n- After sign-in, the dashboard shows:\n  - the user's first and last name;\n  - the account name, **Current Account**;\n  - the five-digit public account number;\n  - the GBP balance; and\n  - Deposit, Withdraw, and Transfer actions.\n- The application is one SPA with client-side routes:\n  - `/sign-in`\n  - `/account`\n  - `/account/deposit`\n  - `/account/withdraw`\n  - `/account/transfer`\n  - `/account/transfer/confirm`\n  - `/session-expired`\n- Deposit, withdrawal, and transfer use separate views rather than modals.\n- The interface covers loading, zero balance, invalid amount, insufficient funds, recipient not found, success, expired session, and unexpected server error states.\n- Transactions are persisted but are not displayed in the interface.\n- The interface targets ordinary desktop web browsers only.\n- After a successful operation, return to `/account` and show a dismissible success banner.\n- A deposit or withdrawal banner includes the formatted amount and result.\n- A transfer banner also includes the recipient's name and five-digit account number.\n- Refreshing the dashboard never repeats an operation.\n- The desktop dashboard is one spacious central account panel with no sidebar, transaction list, charts, or secondary banking statistics.\n- The header contains Banking App and Sign out.\n- The panel greets the user, then shows Current Account, the user's name, account number, a prominent formatted balance, and three large action buttons.\n- Operation views retain the minimal header, include a clear Back to account action, and use one large primary submit button.\n- Navigating away before submission discards unsent form state.\n- Deposit and withdrawal submit directly after validation.\n- Only transfer has a separate review-and-confirm step.\n- Refreshing the transfer confirmation route discards its temporary preview and redirects to `/account/transfer` with a request to review again.\n- Normal Back navigation from confirmation preserves the current transfer form in React navigation state.\n\n### Transfer journey\n\nTransfer is a three-step flow:\n\n1. Enter the five-digit destination account number and amount.\n2. Review the recipient's first and last name, account number, formatted amount, and a warning that transferred funds may not be recoverable.\n3. Confirm and submit the transfer.\n\nThe sender does not type the recipient's name. It is fetched for confirmation. The server revalidates the recipient inside the final database transaction; the preview is never authoritative.\n\n## Money model\n\n- The application supports GBP only.\n- The pound symbol is part of the presentation.\n- There is deliberately no currency-code column.\n- Monetary values use currency-agnostic names:\n  - `balance_minor`\n  - `amount_minor`\n- The minor unit is pence for this GBP application.\n- PostgreSQL stores balances and amounts as `BIGINT`.\n- The server represents minor-unit values as decimal strings in JSON, for example:\n\n  ```json\n  {\n    \"balanceMinor\": \"125034\"\n  }\n  ```\n\n- The client formats the example above as `£1,250.34`.\n- The client submits human-readable decimal input as a string. The server parses it into pence without floating-point multiplication.\n- Deposit, withdrawal, and transfer amounts must be positive and have at most two decimal places.\n- Withdrawals and transfers cannot exceed the available balance.\n- Transfers to the same account are rejected.\n- No artificial maximum operation amount is imposed in this exercise.\n\n### Balance strategy\n\n`accounts.balance_minor` is the authoritative current balance. Each operation updates the balance and inserts its transaction record atomically in the same PostgreSQL transaction.\n\nThis avoids recalculating the balance by summing the entire transaction history on every request. A full append-only ledger with reconciliation would provide stronger auditability but would add complexity beyond this exercise.\n\n## Data model\n\n### `users`\n\n- internal UUID primary key;\n- unique email;\n- first name;\n- last name;\n- password hash data; and\n- creation timestamp.\n\n### `sessions`\n\n- internal UUID primary key;\n- owning user;\n- hash of the opaque session token;\n- last activity timestamp;\n- absolute expiry timestamp; and\n- creation timestamp.\n\n### `accounts`\n\n- internal UUID primary key;\n- owning user, unique because each user has one account;\n- fixed five-digit demo account number with a unique constraint;\n- balance in minor units with a non-negative constraint; and\n- creation timestamp.\n\nAccount numbers range from `10000` through `99999`. The seed uses fixed fixture numbers; PostgreSQL enforces uniqueness.\n\n### `money_transactions`\n\n- internal UUID primary key;\n- unique UUID idempotency key;\n- type: deposit, withdrawal, or transfer;\n- positive amount in minor units;\n- nullable source account;\n- nullable destination account;\n- initiating user; and\n- resulting initiator balance in minor units, allowing an identical idempotent retry to reproduce its original result; and\n- creation timestamp.\n\nDatabase constraints enforce the valid shape of each transaction:\n\n- deposit: no source and one destination;\n- withdrawal: one source and no destination;\n- transfer: one source, one different destination;\n- amount must be positive.\n\nTransactions are retained for auditability and verification even though they are not shown in the UI.\n\nSeeded opening balances also receive matching deposit transaction records so every initial balance has an audit explanation.\n\n## Transaction safety and concurrency\n\nEvery money operation uses one checked-out `pg` client and an explicit PostgreSQL transaction:\n\n1. begin;\n2. acquire row locks for every affected account;\n3. validate ownership, recipient, and available funds;\n4. insert the transaction record;\n5. update affected balances; and\n6. commit.\n\nAny failure rolls back every change, so a partial or unfinished transfer cannot remain in the database. A database or server failure before commit also rolls the operation back.\n\nUse PostgreSQL's `READ COMMITTED` isolation with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`. A transfer locks both accounts in ascending internal-ID order to avoid lost updates, overdrafts, and common deadlock patterns.\n\n### Idempotency\n\nThe browser generates a `crypto.randomUUID()` for each deliberate deposit, withdrawal, or transfer submission. It reuses that key when retrying the same request. PostgreSQL enforces uniqueness, preventing a network retry or duplicate submission from moving money twice.\n\nA timestamp, user identifier, or account identifier is not included in the key. Those values are predictable, may expose information in logs, and do not improve on a cryptographically random UUID.\n\nIf an idempotency key is reused with the identical operation, return its original result without moving money again. If the same key is reused with a different operation type, amount, source, or recipient, return `409 IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT`.\n\n## Authentication and session security\n\n### Passwords\n\n- There is no registration flow.\n- The database is seeded with three users for demonstration and authorization tests.\n- Seeded passwords are stored using Node's stable, built-in asynchronous `scrypt` implementation.\n- Each password receives a unique random 16-byte salt.\n- Initial `scrypt` parameters are `N = 2^17`, `r = 8`, and `p = 1`, with an explicit memory allowance large enough for that configuration.\n- Verification uses a timing-safe comparison.\n- Passwords are never stored in plaintext or written to logs.\n- Demo plaintext credentials are documented in the README so reviewers can sign in.\n\nBuilt-in asynchronous scrypt provides memory-hard password derivation without an additional native dependency. Parameters should be measured on the deployment runtime.\n\n### Sessions\n\n- Use an opaque, cryptographically random session token rather than a JWT.\n- Send the token in an `HttpOnly`, `SameSite=Lax` cookie.\n- Use the `Secure` cookie flag whenever HTTPS is available; local HTTP cannot use it.\n- Store only a SHA-256 hash of the high-entropy token in PostgreSQL.\n- Logout deletes the server-side session immediately.\n- Sessions have an eight-hour absolute lifetime.\n- Ten minutes of user inactivity ends the session.\n- The client tracks keyboard, pointer, touch, and relevant page activity. Activity restarts the idle timer.\n- While the user remains active, the client sends an authenticated heartbeat at most once every two minutes so the server can update `last_activity_at`.\n- Both client and server enforce expiry.\n- An expired authenticated request correctly returns HTTP `401`; the client handles it by clearing sensitive state and navigating to `/session-expired`.\n- The expiry screen explains that the user was logged out for security after more than ten minutes of inactivity.\n\nDatabase-backed sessions were chosen over JWTs because this is one server with an existing database. Server-side sessions support immediate revocation and straightforward logout. JWTs would add signing, claims, expiry, and refresh or revocation complexity without providing a useful statelessness benefit here.\n\n## Authorization and request security\n\n- The server derives the authenticated user from the session; browser-supplied user IDs never establish identity.\n- Users can only view or modify the account attached to their authenticated user.\n- Parameterized `pg` queries are used instead of concatenating or “sanitizing” SQL values.\n- Any dynamic SQL identifier must come from a fixed allowlist.\n- Zod validates request bodies, parameters, and expected formats.\n- Request body size is limited.\n- Mutating requests receive an Origin check as CSRF defence.\n- Authentication responses do not reveal whether an email exists.\n- Passwords, cookies, raw session tokens, and sensitive request bodies are not logged.\n- Sign-in is rate-limited to five failed attempts per email/IP per 15 minutes.\n- Recipient lookup and transfer attempts are limited to 20 per authenticated user per minute.\n- An in-memory limiter is acceptable for the single local Express process.\n- Vite proxies same-origin `/api` requests to Express during development.\n- Do not enable permissive CORS.\n- Use Helmet for standard HTTP security headers.\n\nEndpoint names remain clear and conventional. Obscure URLs are not a security control; authentication, authorization, rate limiting, CSRF controls, and careful response disclosure protect the operations.\n\n### Error disclosure\n\nExpected failures use a consistent error object with a stable machine-readable code and a safe user-facing message.\n\n| Situation | Disclosure policy |\n| --- | --- |\n| Unknown email or incorrect password | Return the same `AUTHENTICATION_FAILED` response. |\n| Expired session | Return `SESSION_EXPIRED`; it describes only the caller's session. |\n| Invalid request value | Return safe, field-specific validation errors. |\n| Missing, invalid, or unusable recipient | Return generic `RECIPIENT_UNAVAILABLE`. |\n| Insufficient funds | Return `INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS`; it describes only the caller's account. |\n| Resource the caller cannot access | Return `404` instead of revealing its existence or owner. |\n| Unexpected server or database error | Return a generic message and a non-sensitive request ID; never expose SQL, stack traces, or internal identifiers. |\n\n## Module decisions\n\n- Express routes own HTTP parsing, status codes, cookies, and response mapping.\n- Money behaviour lives behind one small module interface with deposit, withdrawal, transfer preview, and transfer operations.\n- The money module owns authorization, account locking, operation validation, transaction-record insertion, and balance updates.\n- Route handlers do not contain SQL transaction logic.\n- Native `fetch` performs browser requests.\n- React context holds authenticated-session state.\n- Forms use local React state.\n- Do not add Redux or a server-state library without a demonstrated need.\n\n## HTTP interface\n\nUse this endpoint surface:\n\n```text\nPOST /api/auth/sign-in\nPOST /api/auth/sign-out\nGET  /api/auth/session\nPOST /api/auth/heartbeat\n\nGET  /api/account\nPOST /api/deposits\nPOST /api/withdrawals\nPOST /api/transfers/preview\nPOST /api/transfers\n```\n\nThe authenticated user and source account always come from the server-side session. Browser requests never supply them as proof of identity.\n\nTransfer preview uses `POST`, keeping the destination account number in a validated request body rather than a URL or ordinary access log.\n\nSuccessful deposit and withdrawal responses include the resulting `balanceMinor` and applied `amountMinor`. A successful transfer additionally includes only the recipient's display name and public account number. The resulting balance means the sender's balance after the committed operation; the amount means the amount moved by that operation.\n\nInternal user IDs and account UUIDs are not returned. An idempotent retry returns the original successful result.\n\n## Input behaviour\n\n- Money fields use a text input with `inputMode=\"decimal\"` and a fixed visual `£` prefix.\n- Accept digits and at most one decimal point with no more than two decimal places.\n- Preserve the entered string while editing and format it to two decimal places on blur.\n- Reject commas, exponential notation, negative values, and zero.\n- Client validation improves feedback; identical server validation remains authoritative.\n- Recipient account-number fields use `inputMode=\"numeric\"`, accept exactly five digits, and have `maxLength={5}`.\n- Reject spaces and non-digits rather than silently rewriting the identifier.\n\n## Seed data\n\nCreate these three fictional users:\n\n| User | Account number | Opening balance |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Alex Morgan | 48271 | £2,450.75 |\n| Jamie Chen | 61504 | £890.20 |\n| Samira Patel | 93826 | £12,300.00 |\n\nAlex is the primary README login. The documented demo credentials are:\n\n```text\nalex.morgan@example.test   / Demo-Alex!2026\njamie.chen@example.test    / Demo-Jamie!2026\nsamira.patel@example.test  / Demo-Samira!2026\n```\n\nOnly their scrypt hashes are stored in PostgreSQL.\n\n## Logging\n\n- Assign a non-sensitive request ID to each request.\n- Request logs contain only request ID, HTTP method, matched route, response status, and duration.\n- Unexpected server-side exception details are correlated through the request ID.\n- Never log request bodies, email addresses, account numbers, amounts, cookies, session tokens, passwords, password hashes, or SQL parameters.\n- `money_transactions` is the operation audit record; application request logs are not a financial ledger.\n\n## Testing decisions\n\n- To save setup and maintenance time in this local exercise, backend integration tests reuse the configured application database.\n- A dedicated test database with separately configured credentials and reset protection is the intended improvement. The shared setup is not appropriate for real production data.\n- Tests restore fixture state, and successful test commands rebuild and reseed the configured public schema. Do not run the app or database-backed suites concurrently. `db:reset` is destructive and currently has no database-name allowlist.\n- Integration tests exercise the Express interface through Supertest while retaining real PostgreSQL constraints and transaction behaviour.\n- React Testing Library covers sign-in, amount validation, transfer confirmation, and session expiry.\n- Playwright provides a small end-to-end suite against the running client, server, and configured database.\n- Playwright journey one signs in, deposits, withdraws, and verifies the updated balance.\n- Playwright journey two transfers from Alex to Jamie, signs in as Jamie, and verifies the received balance.\n- Playwright journey three verifies declined transfers leave money unchanged.\n- Backend integration tests remain the priority because authorization, locking, rollback, and balance correctness are the highest-risk behaviours.\n\nEssential backend cases include:\n\n- unauthenticated access rejection;\n- cross-user access and debit rejection;\n- malformed, zero, and negative money rejection;\n- insufficient-funds rejection;\n- atomic balance updates and one record per successful transfer;\n- rollback with no balance change after a failed transfer;\n- same-account transfer rejection;\n- duplicate-idempotency protection;\n- concurrent-withdrawal overdraft protection;\n- deadlock-free opposite-direction transfers;\n- expired and inactive session rejection;\n- sign-in and recipient rate limits; and\n- database constraints rejecting invalid transaction shapes.\n\n## Explicit trade-offs\n\n### One account per user\n\nChosen because the assignment only requires a user to access their own account. It keeps authorization and the interface clear.\n\nFuture improvement: support multiple accounts per user, account selection, user-defined account names, and different account types.\n\n### GBP only and no currency code\n\nChosen to keep the exercise focused. Generic minor-unit naming preserves a useful conceptual seam, but the implementation and display are GBP-specific.\n\nFuture improvement: attach a currency to each account, format from currency metadata, and define or deliberately reject cross-currency transfers. Supporting multiple currencies does not automatically imply supporting conversion.\n\n### Five-digit public account numbers\n\nChosen for a short, readable demo identifier. The small 90,000-value space is enumerable and unsuitable for production despite random generation and a uniqueness constraint.\n\nFuture improvement: use a substantially larger public identifier space and production-grade abuse controls.\n\n### Stored balance plus transaction records\n\nChosen for efficient balance reads and straightforward implementation. Balance updates and records remain atomic.\n\nFuture improvement: introduce an append-only double-entry ledger, derived or reconciled balances, and immutable audit controls.\n\n### No transaction-history interface\n\nChosen because the assignment does not request it. Records still exist in PostgreSQL.\n\nFuture improvement: display paginated account activity with credit/debit direction derived from whether the current account is the source or destination.\n\n### No registration\n\nChosen because registration is not requested and would distract from money correctness and authorization.\n\nFuture improvement: registration, email verification, password reset, and account recovery.\n\n### No maximum operation amount\n\nChosen to avoid inventing an unsupported business rule. The database range, positive-value validation, and available-balance checks still apply.\n\nFuture improvement: configurable per-operation, daily, and risk-based limits.\n\n### Local in-memory rate limiting\n\nChosen because the assignment runs one local Express process.\n\nFuture improvement: use a shared rate-limit store such as Redis when running multiple instances.\n\n### Local HTTP\n\nChosen because cloud deployment and production infrastructure are explicitly excluded.\n\nFuture improvement: HTTPS, secure-cookie enforcement, HSTS, and secret management.\n\n### Desktop-only interface\n\nChosen to keep the visual implementation focused on the requested local demonstration.\n\nThe banking workflow targets desktop browsers. The reviewer guide also supports narrow screens.\n\n## Visual tokens\n\n```css\n--color-background: #fbfbfa;\n--color-surface: #eceff0;\n--color-text: #111816;\n--color-accent: #08c6ed;\n--color-border: #202624;\n--color-danger: #b42318;\n```\n\nCyan is reserved for important actions and focus states. Error red communicates validation failures.\n\n## Security references\n\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Node.js 24 Crypto documentation](https://nodejs.org/download/release/latest-v24.x/docs/api/crypto.html)\n";

export const specificationDocument =
  '# Banking App — Implementation Specification\n\n## 1. Objective\n\nBuild a local desktop banking SPA as a scoped exercise. A seeded user can sign in, view one GBP current account, make simulated deposits and withdrawals, and transfer money to another seeded account.\n\nPriorities, in order:\n\n1. server-side authorization;\n2. atomic and concurrency-safe money movement;\n3. secure, revocable sessions;\n4. meaningful security-focused tests; and\n5. code that is small enough to explain confidently.\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- registration or account recovery;\n- multiple accounts per user;\n- multiple currencies or conversion;\n- transaction-history UI;\n- real payment providers;\n- mobile/responsive layouts;\n- cloud deployment; and\n- production identity or infrastructure controls.\n\nSee the decision record for reasoning, rejected alternatives, trade-offs, and future improvements.\n\n## 2. Stack and repository\n\n- Node.js 24\n- npm workspaces\n- React, TypeScript, Vite, and React Router\n- Express and TypeScript\n- Zod\n- PostgreSQL through `pg` and parameterized SQL\n- Vitest, Supertest, and React Testing Library\n- Playwright\n- Plain CSS\n\n```text\nbanking-app/\n├── client/\n├── server/\n├── database/\n│   ├── migrations/\n│   └── scripts/\n├── README.md\n├── .env.example\n├── .nvmrc\n└── package.json\n```\n\nDefault local configuration:\n\n| Concern                                              | Value                    |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| Vite                                                 | `http://localhost:47831` |\n| Express                                              | `http://localhost:47832` |\n| PostgreSQL                                           | `localhost:47833`        |\n| Configured application database (also used by tests) | `banking_app`            |\n\nAll ports and connection values are overridable through environment variables.\n\n## 3. Commands and database lifecycle\n\nPrimary reviewer workflow:\n\n```text\nnpm install\nnpm run db:setup\nnpm run dev\nnpm test\nnpm run test:e2e\n```\n\n| Command              | Behaviour                                                 |\n| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `npm run db:create`  | Attempt to create the configured database.                |\n| `npm run db:migrate` | Apply unapplied numbered SQL migrations.                  |\n| `npm run db:seed`    | Insert missing demo fixtures.                             |\n| `npm run db:reset`   | Rebuild the configured public schema, migrate, then seed. |\n| `npm run db:setup`   | Create the configured database, migrate, and seed.        |\n\nThe migration runner records successful files in `schema_migrations` and applies every migration transactionally. Express never migrates automatically. Startup checks connectivity and required schema, then gives a helpful `db:setup` instruction if setup is missing.\n\nTo save setup time for this local exercise, the app and database-backed tests use the same configured application database. A dedicated test database and guarded reset target are future improvements. `db:reset` drops and recreates the configured public schema without a database-name allowlist; use disposable demo data, and do not run the app or database-backed suites concurrently.\n\n## 4. Seed data\n\n| Name         | Email                       | Password           | Account | Opening balance |\n| ------------ | --------------------------- | ------------------ | ------: | --------------: |\n| Alex Morgan  | `alex.morgan@example.test`  | `Demo-Alex!2026`   |   48271 |       £2,450.75 |\n| Jamie Chen   | `jamie.chen@example.test`   | `Demo-Jamie!2026`  |   61504 |         £890.20 |\n| Samira Patel | `samira.patel@example.test` | `Demo-Samira!2026` |   93826 |      £12,300.00 |\n\nAlex is the primary README account. Store only scrypt password digests. Each opening balance has a corresponding seeded deposit transaction.\n\n## 5. Database contract\n\nAll timestamps use `TIMESTAMPTZ`. Application code generates internal UUIDs. Foreign keys and lookup columns have appropriate indexes.\n\n### `users`\n\n| Column            | Requirements                                          |\n| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`              | UUID primary key                                      |\n| `email`           | Canonical lowercase text, unique, not null            |\n| `first_name`      | Non-empty text, not null                              |\n| `last_name`       | Non-empty text, not null                              |\n| `password_digest` | Encoded scrypt parameters, salt, and digest; not null |\n| `created_at`      | Not null, defaults to current time                    |\n\n### `sessions`\n\n| Column             | Requirements                       |\n| ------------------ | ---------------------------------- |\n| `id`               | UUID primary key                   |\n| `user_id`          | User foreign key, not null         |\n| `token_hash`       | SHA-256 bytes, unique, not null    |\n| `last_activity_at` | Not null                           |\n| `expires_at`       | Absolute expiry, not null          |\n| `created_at`       | Not null, defaults to current time |\n\n### `accounts`\n\n| Column           | Requirements                                       |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`             | UUID primary key                                   |\n| `user_id`        | User foreign key, unique, not null                 |\n| `account_number` | Text, unique, exactly five digits from 10000–99999 |\n| `balance_minor`  | `BIGINT`, not null, check `>= 0`                   |\n| `created_at`     | Not null, defaults to current time                 |\n\nThere is one account per user and no currency column. All money is GBP; all minor-unit values are pence.\n\n### `money_transactions`\n\n| Column                   | Requirements                                         |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`                     | UUID primary key                                     |\n| `idempotency_key`        | UUID, unique, not null                               |\n| `type`                   | `deposit`, `withdrawal`, or `transfer`               |\n| `amount_minor`           | `BIGINT`, not null, check `> 0`                      |\n| `source_account_id`      | Nullable account foreign key                         |\n| `destination_account_id` | Nullable account foreign key                         |\n| `initiated_by_user_id`   | User foreign key, not null                           |\n| `balance_after_minor`    | Initiator\'s resulting `BIGINT` balance, check `>= 0` |\n| `created_at`             | Not null, defaults to current time                   |\n\nDatabase checks enforce:\n\n- deposit: null source and non-null destination;\n- withdrawal: non-null source and null destination;\n- transfer: non-null, different source and destination; and\n- positive amounts and non-negative resulting balances.\n\nTransaction records exist for auditability and idempotent response recovery but are not displayed.\n\n## 6. Money representation\n\n- `balance_minor` and `amount_minor` are generic names; their values are pence here.\n- PostgreSQL stores amounts as `BIGINT`.\n- JSON represents minor units as base-10 strings to prevent JavaScript precision loss.\n- Browser requests send a decimal string such as `"12.34"`.\n- The server converts decimals to integer pence without floating-point multiplication.\n- Accept digits, an optional decimal point, and no more than two fractional digits.\n- Reject zero, negatives, signs, commas, whitespace, exponent notation, and malformed decimals.\n- Do not impose an artificial maximum operation amount.\n- Withdrawals and transfers cannot exceed the available balance.\n- Transfers to the current account are rejected.\n\n## 7. Transaction, concurrency, and idempotency\n\nEvery deposit, withdrawal, and transfer uses one checked-out `pg` client and one explicit PostgreSQL transaction under `READ COMMITTED` isolation.\n\n1. Begin.\n2. Resolve and lock every affected account with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`.\n3. For transfers, acquire both locks in ascending internal account-ID order.\n4. Check the idempotency key again after acquiring relevant locks.\n5. Revalidate session-derived ownership, recipient, amount, and available funds.\n6. Calculate resulting balances.\n7. Insert one transaction record containing the initiator\'s resulting balance.\n8. Update all affected balances.\n9. Commit.\n\nAny failure rolls back every balance change and transaction record.\n\nIdempotency rules:\n\n- The browser creates `crypto.randomUUID()` once per deliberate submission.\n- Retrying the identical operation reuses its key and returns the recorded successful result without moving money.\n- Reusing a key with a different type, amount, source, or destination returns `409 IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT`.\n- A concurrent duplicate that loses the unique-key race rolls back and resolves the committed result safely.\n\n## 8. Authentication and sessions\n\n### Passwords\n\n- Use asynchronous Node `crypto.scrypt`.\n- Generate a unique random 16-byte salt.\n- Use `N = 2^17`, `r = 8`, `p = 1`, with sufficient explicit `maxmem`.\n- Encode algorithm, parameters, salt, and digest into `password_digest`.\n- Compare derived digests with `timingSafeEqual`.\n- Perform a dummy hash for unknown emails so timing does not trivially reveal account existence.\n- Never log passwords, salts, or digests.\n\n### Opaque sessions\n\n- Generate a high-entropy random token.\n- Send it in a `banking_app_session` cookie.\n- Cookie settings: `HttpOnly`, `SameSite=Lax`, path `/`, eight-hour maximum age, and `Secure` whenever HTTPS is enabled.\n- Store only its SHA-256 hash.\n- Derive the current user and account only from a valid server-side session.\n- Explicit sign-out deletes the session and clears the cookie.\n- Absolute lifetime: eight hours.\n- Inactivity timeout: ten minutes.\n\nThe client tracks keyboard, pointer, touch, and page-visibility activity. Activity restarts its idle timer. While active, it sends at most one heartbeat every two minutes. The server remains authoritative for both expiries.\n\nOn expiry HTTP `401`, clear sensitive client state and navigate to `/session-expired`. That page explains the security logout after ten minutes of inactivity. Explicit sign-out returns to `/sign-in`.\n\n## 9. HTTP and security controls\n\nVite proxies same-origin `/api` traffic to Express. Do not enable permissive CORS. Validate `Origin` on all state-changing requests, including sign-in. Use Helmet, body-size limits, Zod, and parameterized SQL.\n\n### Authentication\n\n```text\nPOST /api/auth/sign-in\nbody: { "email": string, "password": string }\nresult: { "user": { "firstName": string, "lastName": string } }\n\nPOST /api/auth/sign-out\nresult: 204\n\nGET /api/auth/session\nresult: { "user": { "firstName": string, "lastName": string } }\n\nPOST /api/auth/heartbeat\nresult: 204\n```\n\n### Account\n\n```text\nGET /api/account\nresult: {\n  "account": {\n    "holderName": string,\n    "accountNumber": string,\n    "balanceMinor": string\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Deposit and withdrawal\n\n```text\nPOST /api/deposits\nPOST /api/withdrawals\nbody: { "amount": string, "idempotencyKey": UUID }\nresult: {\n  "result": {\n    "balanceMinor": string,\n    "amountMinor": string\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Transfer\n\n```text\nPOST /api/transfers/preview\nbody: { "recipientAccountNumber": string, "amount": string }\nresult: {\n  "preview": {\n    "amountMinor": string,\n    "recipient": { "name": string, "accountNumber": string }\n  }\n}\n\nPOST /api/transfers\nbody: {\n  "recipientAccountNumber": string,\n  "amount": string,\n  "idempotencyKey": UUID\n}\nresult: {\n  "result": {\n    "balanceMinor": string,\n    "amountMinor": string,\n    "recipient": { "name": string, "accountNumber": string }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe final transfer re-resolves and locks the destination. Preview state never authorizes or determines the committed destination.\n\n### Errors and disclosure\n\n```json\n{\n  "error": {\n    "code": "INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS",\n    "message": "Your account does not have enough available funds.",\n    "fields": {}\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`fields` is optional and contains only safe validation feedback.\n\n| Situation                                           | Status and code                      |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| Unknown email or wrong password                     | `401 AUTHENTICATION_FAILED`          |\n| No usable session credential                        | `401 AUTHENTICATION_REQUIRED`        |\n| Recognized session past its idle or absolute expiry | `401 SESSION_EXPIRED`                |\n| Invalid input                                       | `400 VALIDATION_ERROR`               |\n| Unusable recipient                                  | `404 RECIPIENT_UNAVAILABLE`          |\n| Insufficient funds                                  | `409 INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS`             |\n| Different operation reusing a key                   | `409 IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT`           |\n| Inaccessible resource                               | non-revealing `404`                  |\n| Unexpected failure                                  | `500 INTERNAL_ERROR` with request ID |\n\nDo not return internal UUIDs, SQL, stack traces, ownership details, or sensitive configuration. Clear endpoint names are acceptable; obscurity is not an authorization control.\n\n### Rate limiting and logging\n\n- Sign-in: five failed attempts per canonical email/IP per 15 minutes.\n- Recipient preview and transfer: 20 combined attempts per authenticated user per minute.\n- Use an in-memory limiter for the single local process.\n- Give each request a non-sensitive request ID.\n- Log request ID, method, matched route, status, and duration only.\n- Correlate unexpected exception details server-side by request ID.\n- Never log bodies, emails, account numbers, amounts, cookies, tokens, passwords, hashes, or SQL parameters.\n\n## 10. Module interfaces\n\nExpress routes own HTTP parsing, cookies, status codes, and response mapping. They delegate money behaviour to one module:\n\n```ts\ndeposit(input): Promise<MoneyResult>\nwithdraw(input): Promise<MoneyResult>\npreviewTransfer(input): Promise<TransferPreview>\ntransfer(input): Promise<TransferResult>\n```\n\nInputs receive authenticated identity from session middleware, never from request-body identity fields. The module owns authorization, row locking, validation, idempotency, audit records, and balance changes.\n\nReact uses native `fetch`, one context for authenticated-session state, and local state for forms. Do not add Redux or a server-state library.\n\n## 11. User interface\n\n```css\n--color-background: #fbfbfa;\n--color-surface: #eceff0;\n--color-text: #111816;\n--color-accent: #08c6ed;\n--color-border: #202624;\n--color-danger: #b42318;\n```\n\nUse a spacious desktop layout, near-black typography, pale-grey surfaces, rounded controls, and cyan primary actions. Use native labels, inputs, and buttons. The reviewer guide additionally supports narrow screens.\n\nRoutes:\n\n```text\n/sign-in\n/account\n/account/deposit\n/account/withdraw\n/account/transfer\n/account/transfer/confirm\n/session-expired\n```\n\nUnauthenticated account routes navigate to sign-in.\n\n### Dashboard\n\n- Minimal header with Banking App and Sign out.\n- Greeting using the first name.\n- One large Current Account panel.\n- Holder name and five-digit account number.\n- Prominent GBP balance.\n- Three large buttons: Deposit, Withdraw, Transfer.\n- No history, sidebar, charts, or secondary statistics.\n\n### Money input\n\n- Text input with `inputMode="decimal"` and visual `£` prefix.\n- Preserve the editing string and format to two decimals on blur.\n- Client validation mirrors server rules for feedback; the server remains authoritative.\n\n### Deposit and withdrawal\n\n- Separate route with minimal header, Back to account, one amount field, and one large submit button.\n- No confirmation step.\n- Disable repeat submission while pending.\n- On success, return to `/account` with a dismissible result banner.\n\n### Transfer\n\n1. Enter exactly five recipient digits and an amount.\n2. Request preview.\n3. Show recipient name, account number, formatted amount, and a warning that funds may not be recoverable.\n4. Confirm once using a new idempotency key.\n5. Disable repeat confirmation while pending.\n6. On success, return to `/account` with recipient and amount in the banner.\n\nRefreshing confirmation returns to transfer with “Please review the transfer again.” Normal Back preserves form values through React navigation state. Do not persist unfinished transfers in PostgreSQL or browser storage.\n\nRequired states: loading, zero balance, invalid amount, insufficient funds, recipient unavailable, success, session expired, and unexpected failure.\n\n## 12. Testing acceptance criteria\n\nSupertest integration tests run against the configured application database with restored demo fixtures and cover:\n\n- authentication, logout, revocation, expiry, and inactivity;\n- indistinguishable unknown-email and wrong-password responses;\n- unauthenticated and cross-user rejection;\n- money-input edge cases and exact-cent behaviour;\n- insufficient funds and non-negative balances;\n- atomic transfer and rollback;\n- matching transaction records;\n- same-account rejection;\n- identical idempotent retry and conflicting reuse;\n- concurrent withdrawals never overdrawing;\n- opposite-direction transfers completing without deadlock;\n- rate limits;\n- invalid transaction shapes rejected by database constraints; and\n- internal errors not leaking details.\n\nReact Testing Library covers sign-in, money validation, transfer confirmation, pending-button protection, expected errors, success navigation, and session-expiry navigation.\n\nPlaywright journeys:\n\n1. Alex signs in, deposits, withdraws, and sees the correct final balance.\n2. Alex transfers to Jamie, then Jamie signs in and sees the received balance.\n3. Alex receives explanations for declined transfers and the balances remain unchanged.\n\n## 13. Acceptance summary\n\nThe application is complete when:\n\n- a reviewer can configure and run it from the documented commands;\n- all assignment operations work through the interface;\n- authorization prevents cross-user access and modification;\n- committed operations update balances and records atomically;\n- concurrent and duplicate requests cannot create or lose money;\n- sessions, validation, rate limits, disclosure, and logs follow this contract;\n- the security-focused tests pass against real PostgreSQL;\n- the desktop interface uses the documented visual styles; and\n- the README explains the implementation and production improvements.\n';

export const questionsDocument =
  "# Banking App — Engineering Questions and Answers\n\n62 selected questions from the design discussion, with answers aligned to the current implementation.\n\n## Scope and product\n\n### Q1 — How many accounts should each user have?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** One account per user\n- **B.** Multiple accounts per user\n- **C.** No persisted accounts\n\n**Chosen: A — One account per user**\n\nOne Current Account per user. PostgreSQL enforces a unique user reference on accounts. Multiple accounts would require account selection and additional ownership checks.\n\n### Q2 — Which banking operations are in scope?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Balance viewing only\n- **B.** Deposits and withdrawals\n- **C.** Balance, deposits, withdrawals and transfers\n\n**Chosen: C — Balance, deposits, withdrawals and transfers**\n\nBalance viewing, simulated deposits and withdrawals, and transfers between seeded accounts. The application focuses on authentication and correct money movement.\n\n### Q3 — Should the application include registration?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Self-service registration\n- **B.** Administrator-created accounts\n- **C.** Seeded demo users\n\n**Chosen: C — Seeded demo users**\n\nThe demo uses seeded fictional users. Registration, email verification and account recovery are outside the implemented product scope.\n\n### Q4 — Should transactions be displayed to the user?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Full transaction history\n- **B.** Recent activity only\n- **C.** Persist records without a history screen\n\n**Chosen: C — Persist records without a history screen**\n\nSuccessful operations are recorded in money_transactions, but there is no transaction-history interface. The records support verification and idempotent response recovery.\n\n### Q5 — How should deposits and withdrawals be described?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Imply real payment rails\n- **B.** Label every action as fake\n- **C.** Use normal banking language and document the simulation\n\n**Chosen: C — Use normal banking language and document the simulation**\n\nThey are simulated banking operations, not connections to external payment providers. Transfers move balances within this application.\n\n### Q6 — What user information should be stored and shown?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Email only\n- **B.** One display-name field\n- **C.** Email plus separate first and last names\n\n**Chosen: C — Email plus separate first and last names**\n\nEmail, first name and last name are stored. The interface shows the account holder’s full name, and the transfer preview shows the resolved recipient’s name for confirmation.\n\n## Currency and money\n\n### Q7 — Should the data model support multiple currencies now?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Multiple currencies\n- **B.** A currency column fixed to GBP\n- **C.** GBP-only behavior without a currency column\n\n**Chosen: C — GBP-only behavior without a currency column**\n\nThe application supports GBP only, with no currency column. Multiple currencies would require explicit currency rules and validation across accounts and transfers.\n\n### Q8 — How should currency appear in the interface?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** ISO code only\n- **B.** Pound symbol only\n- **C.** Both GBP and £ everywhere\n\n**Chosen: B — Pound symbol only**\n\nAmounts use the pound symbol and two decimal places. The currency presentation remains separate from integer minor-unit storage.\n\n### Q9 — How should monetary database fields be named?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** balance_pence and amount_pence\n- **B.** Decimal balance and amount\n- **C.** Currency-neutral balance_minor and amount_minor\n\n**Chosen: C — Currency-neutral balance_minor and amount_minor**\n\nbalance_minor and amount_minor express the storage unit clearly. In this GBP-only application, those minor units are pence.\n\n### Q10 — How should money be stored?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Floating-point values\n- **B.** PostgreSQL NUMERIC values\n- **C.** Integer minor units in BIGINT\n\n**Chosen: C — Integer minor units in BIGINT**\n\nPostgreSQL BIGINT stores integer minor units. Backend arithmetic uses JavaScript BigInt, avoiding floating-point balance calculations.\n\n### Q11 — How should large integer money values cross JSON?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** JavaScript numbers\n- **B.** Formatted currency strings\n- **C.** Base-10 minor-unit strings\n\n**Chosen: C — Base-10 minor-unit strings**\n\nMinor-unit values are returned as base-10 strings. This avoids precision loss through JavaScript Number and the inability to serialize BigInt directly with JSON.stringify.\n\n### Q12 — How should users enter money?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Number input\n- **B.** Unrestricted free-form text\n- **C.** Text input with decimal input mode and a £ prefix\n\n**Chosen: C — Text input with decimal input mode and a £ prefix**\n\nA text input uses inputMode=\"decimal\" and a visual £ prefix. The entered string is validated without a floating-point conversion.\n\n### Q13 — Which amount formats are valid?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Permissive locale parsing\n- **B.** Automatic sanitization\n- **C.** A narrow positive-decimal grammar\n\n**Chosen: C — A narrow positive-decimal grammar**\n\nA positive decimal string with at most two fractional digits. Zero, negatives, commas, whitespace, exponent notation and malformed decimals are rejected. The backend also checks the PostgreSQL BIGINT range.\n\n### Q14 — Should operations have an artificial maximum amount?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** A fixed demo limit\n- **B.** No additional business cap\n- **C.** Production-style daily and risk limits\n\n**Chosen: B — No additional business cap**\n\nThere is no additional business cap in this demo. Amounts must be positive and within the BIGINT range; withdrawals and transfers require available funds. Credited balances are checked for overflow.\n\n### Q15 — How should the current balance be calculated?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Sum all transactions on every read\n- **B.** Store only a mutable balance\n- **C.** Store the current balance plus atomic transaction records\n\n**Chosen: C — Store the current balance plus atomic transaction records**\n\naccounts.balance_minor is the stored current balance. Every money operation writes its transaction record and balance changes atomically. A double-entry ledger and reconciliation would be additional accounting capabilities.\n\n## Accounts and transfers\n\n### Q16 — What should identify an account publicly?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Internal UUID\n- **B.** Email address\n- **C.** A separate public account number\n\n**Chosen: C — A separate public account number**\n\nA separate five-digit account number is used in the interface. Internal relationships and lock ordering use UUIDs, which are not returned in ordinary account responses.\n\n### Q17 — What format should public account numbers use?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** UUID\n- **B.** Alphanumeric identifier\n- **C.** Exactly five decimal digits\n\n**Chosen: C — Exactly five decimal digits**\n\nExactly five decimal digits from 10000 to 99999. The client, API and database independently validate that format.\n\n### Q18 — How should account-number uniqueness be enforced?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Application check only\n- **B.** Generated sequence without a constraint\n- **C.** Fixed demo numbers backed by database uniqueness\n\n**Chosen: C — Fixed demo numbers backed by database uniqueness**\n\nThe database has a UNIQUE constraint. The current seed uses fixed account numbers for repeatable demonstrations; it does not implement random allocation with collision retries.\n\n### Q19 — Is a five-digit identifier production-ready?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Treat five digits as production-ready\n- **B.** Expand the identifier now\n- **C.** Accept it as a documented demo constraint\n\n**Chosen: C — Accept it as a documented demo constraint**\n\nIt is a deliberate demo constraint with a small, enumerable identifier space. A production design would use a larger space and additional abuse controls.\n\n### Q20 — What account name should appear in the UI?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** User-defined name\n- **B.** No account name\n- **C.** A fixed Current Account label\n\n**Chosen: C — A fixed Current Account label**\n\nThe client displays Current Account. It is presentation text rather than a stored account-name field or a value supplied by the account API.\n\n### Q21 — What must the sender enter for a transfer?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Account number and typed recipient name\n- **B.** Recipient email\n- **C.** Account number and amount\n\n**Chosen: C — Account number and amount**\n\nThe recipient’s account number and an amount. The sender does not supply their own user ID or source-account identity; those are resolved from the authenticated session.\n\n### Q22 — How should the recipient be confirmed?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Submit immediately\n- **B.** Require a typed name match\n- **C.** Resolve and display the recipient before confirmation\n\n**Chosen: C — Resolve and display the recipient before confirmation**\n\nThe preview endpoint resolves the account number and returns the recipient’s display name and amount. The confirmation screen shows those details before submission.\n\n### Q23 — What warning should appear before a transfer?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** No warning\n- **B.** Generic confirmation text\n- **C.** An explicit irrecoverability warning\n\n**Chosen: C — An explicit irrecoverability warning**\n\nThe confirmation screen asks the sender to check the details and warns that transferred funds may not be recoverable.\n\n### Q24 — How many steps should the transfer flow have?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** One-step submission\n- **B.** Form and immediate submission\n- **C.** Entry, review and explicit confirmation\n\n**Chosen: C — Entry, review and explicit confirmation**\n\nEnter account number and amount, review the resolved recipient, then confirm submission. Success returns to the account with a receipt banner.\n\n### Q25 — Can the preview authorize the final transfer?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Trust the client preview\n- **B.** Persist a pending transfer\n- **C.** Re-resolve and validate inside the final transaction\n\n**Chosen: C — Re-resolve and validate inside the final transaction**\n\nNo. Preview data is display-only and does not reserve or check available funds. The final operation resolves accounts again and checks the current balance under row locks.\n\n### Q26 — Should unfinished transfers be persisted?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Persist drafts in PostgreSQL\n- **B.** Persist drafts in browser storage\n- **C.** Keep temporary state in React navigation state\n\n**Chosen: C — Keep temporary state in React navigation state**\n\nForm and preview values are temporary React navigation state. There is no database draft or application-managed persistent browser draft. Confirmation without the required state redirects to the transfer form.\n\n## Authentication and sessions\n\n### Q27 — Should authentication use JWTs or server-side sessions?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** JWT access and refresh tokens\n- **B.** Plaintext database session tokens\n- **C.** Opaque cookie sessions with hashed server tokens\n\n**Chosen: C — Opaque cookie sessions with hashed server tokens**\n\nThe app uses opaque cookie sessions backed by PostgreSQL. A fresh high-entropy token is sent to the browser, and only its SHA-256 hash is stored in the database.\n\n### Q28 — Why not use JWTs here?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Prioritize statelessness\n- **B.** Add refresh and revocation machinery\n- **C.** Use the database already required by the app\n\n**Chosen: C — Use the database already required by the app**\n\nThe application already depends on one database, and database-backed sessions provide simple logout and revocation. JWT refresh and revocation machinery would add complexity without a useful statelessness benefit here.\n\n### Q29 — Which cookie protections should be used?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** JavaScript-readable cookie\n- **B.** Local storage\n- **C.** HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookie\n\n**Chosen: C — HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookie**\n\nThe session cookie is HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax and scoped to /. Its current Secure setting is false for the local HTTP setup. HTTPS and Secure cookies are required for a production deployment.\n\n### Q30 — How long should a session last?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** No expiry\n- **B.** Idle expiry only\n- **C.** Idle and absolute expiry\n\n**Chosen: C — Idle and absolute expiry**\n\nProtected requests enforce ten minutes of inactivity and an eight-hour absolute lifetime. The absolute lifetime is not extended by activity.\n\n### Q31 — What counts as activity?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** API calls only\n- **B.** Mouse clicks only\n- **C.** Relevant keyboard, pointer, touch and visibility activity\n\n**Chosen: C — Relevant keyboard, pointer, touch and visibility activity**\n\nThe client records keyboard, pointer and touch interactions when the page is visible, plus visibility changes back to the page. Authenticated API requests update server activity subject to a one-minute write throttle.\n\n### Q32 — How should the server learn that an active user is still present?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Update on every browser event\n- **B.** Rely only on ordinary API calls\n- **C.** Send a throttled authenticated heartbeat\n\n**Chosen: C — Send a throttled authenticated heartbeat**\n\nThe client checks activity every two minutes and sends an authenticated heartbeat when activity was recent. The server remains authoritative for expiry.\n\n### Q33 — What should happen when inactivity expires?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Return a bare 401\n- **B.** Redirect immediately without explanation\n- **C.** Clear the session and show an expiry explanation\n\n**Chosen: C — Clear the session and show an expiry explanation**\n\nThe server rejects an expired session with a 401 response, deletes the session record and clears the cookie. During authenticated use, the client clears its session state and shows the expiry screen.\n\n### Q34 — How should explicit logout work?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Delete only the browser cookie\n- **B.** Wait for expiry\n- **C.** Delete the server session and clear the cookie\n\n**Chosen: C — Delete the server session and clear the cookie**\n\nThe server deletes the session matching the token hash and clears the cookie. The client clears its session state and navigates to sign-in.\n\n### Q35 — How should passwords be hashed?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Fast SHA hashing\n- **B.** An additional Argon2 dependency\n- **C.** Stable built-in asynchronous scrypt\n\n**Chosen: C — Stable built-in asynchronous scrypt**\n\nAsynchronous Node scrypt derives a digest from a fresh random 16-byte salt. Stored digests include algorithm parameters and salt. Verification uses timingSafeEqual for equal-length digests.\n\n### Q36 — Which scrypt cost should be used?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Library defaults\n- **B.** A low cost optimized for tests\n- **C.** Explicit memory-hard parameters measured per runtime\n\n**Chosen: C — Explicit memory-hard parameters measured per runtime**\n\nThe implementation uses N = 2^17, r = 8, p = 1, a 32-byte derived key and a 256 MiB memory cap. Deployment performance should be measured on the target runtime.\n\n### Q37 — How should unknown users affect sign-in timing and errors?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Return user-not-found\n- **B.** Skip password work\n- **C.** Use the same error and verify against a decoy digest\n\n**Chosen: C — Use the same error and verify against a decoy digest**\n\nUnknown emails and incorrect passwords receive the same failure response. Unknown users still undergo scrypt verification against a decoy digest, reducing a timing signal about account existence.\n\n## Database correctness\n\n### Q38 — Should money operations create transaction records?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Balances only\n- **B.** Application logs only\n- **C.** A dedicated money_transactions table\n\n**Chosen: C — A dedicated money_transactions table**\n\nEvery successful deposit, withdrawal and transfer creates a money_transactions record. Seeded opening balances also receive deposit records.\n\n### Q39 — How should partial or unfinished database operations be prevented?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Independent sequential queries\n- **B.** Compensating updates\n- **C.** One explicit PostgreSQL transaction\n\n**Chosen: C — One explicit PostgreSQL transaction**\n\nOne checked-out pg client runs BEGIN, the record insert and balance updates, then COMMIT. Failure rolls back those writes, and finally releases the connection.\n\n### Q40 — How should concurrent balance changes be protected?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Application mutexes\n- **B.** Serializable isolation for everything\n- **C.** Row locks around affected accounts\n\n**Chosen: C — Row locks around affected accounts**\n\nSELECT ... FOR UPDATE locks affected account rows before funds checks and updates. Operations sharing an account wait their turn. The code uses the connection’s configured isolation level, normally PostgreSQL’s READ COMMITTED default.\n\n### Q41 — How should transfer deadlocks be reduced?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Always lock the sender first\n- **B.** Retry arbitrary deadlocks\n- **C.** Lock both accounts in one stable order\n\n**Chosen: C — Lock both accounts in one stable order**\n\nBoth account rows are locked in ascending internal UUID order. Opposite transfer directions therefore acquire locks in the same order, avoiding the circular wait created by locking each sender first.\n\n### Q42 — Which database constraints back up application validation?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Application validation only\n- **B.** Nullability constraints only\n- **C.** Explicit money, uniqueness, relationship and shape constraints\n\n**Chosen: C — Explicit money, uniqueness, relationship and shape constraints**\n\nBIGINT, NOT NULL, positive-amount and nonnegative-balance checks protect money fields. Unique keys, foreign keys and transaction-shape checks protect relationships. The money module enforces ownership and the correspondence between the amount and balance changes.\n\n### Q43 — What should identify a submitted money operation?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Timestamp and account data\n- **B.** Sequential operation number\n- **C.** Cryptographically random UUID\n\n**Chosen: C — Cryptographically random UUID**\n\nA crypto.randomUUID() idempotency key. The confirmation screen preserves its key while mounted; editing or remounting starts a new operation. Deposit and withdrawal attempts reuse their key while the submitted amount string remains unchanged.\n\n### Q44 — How should retries behave?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Always execute again\n- **B.** Reject every duplicate\n- **C.** Return the original result for an identical retry\n\n**Chosen: C — Return the original result for an identical retry**\n\nA matching key and operation return the original recorded result without applying balances again. If an insert races on the globally unique key, the catch path rolls back, finds the committed record and verifies it before returning a receipt.\n\n### Q45 — What if an idempotency key is reused for different data?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Return the first result\n- **B.** Execute the new request\n- **C.** Reject conflicting reuse\n\n**Chosen: C — Reject conflicting reuse**\n\nReplay comparison checks type, normalized amount, source, destination and initiating user. A mismatch returns 409 IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT. Knowing another operation’s key does not authorize claiming its receipt.\n\n## Database lifecycle\n\n### Q46 — Should Docker run PostgreSQL?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Docker Compose\n- **B.** Embedded database\n- **C.** The existing local PostgreSQL server\n\n**Chosen: C — The existing local PostgreSQL server**\n\nThe project connects to a normal locally running PostgreSQL server. Container orchestration is not required by the implemented setup.\n\n### Q47 — How should schema changes be managed?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Recreate tables during every seed\n- **B.** ORM-generated schema\n- **C.** Numbered SQL migrations\n\n**Chosen: C — Numbered SQL migrations**\n\nNumbered SQL migrations are applied by a small TypeScript runner. Each unapplied file and its migration-history entry are committed together.\n\n### Q48 — Are migrations and seeding the same operation?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Combine structure and fixtures\n- **B.** Keep structure and seed data separate\n- **C.** Migrate automatically at server startup\n\n**Chosen: B — Keep structure and seed data separate**\n\nNo. Migrations manage structure; seeding inserts missing demo fixtures. Setup and reset commands orchestrate these separate steps.\n\n### Q49 — How should applied migrations be tracked?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Rerun every migration file\n- **B.** Infer state from existing tables\n- **C.** Record applied filenames in schema_migrations\n\n**Chosen: C — Record applied filenames in schema_migrations**\n\nschema_migrations stores successfully applied filenames. The runner applies only missing files, in filename order.\n\n### Q50 — How should initial local setup work?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Manual SQL only\n- **B.** Mutate the schema on server startup\n- **C.** Explicit database setup commands\n\n**Chosen: C — Explicit database setup commands**\n\nExplicit db:create, db:migrate, db:seed and db:setup commands prepare the configured database. Express checks readiness on startup rather than migrating automatically.\n\n### Q51 — How do reset scripts behave, and what protection is planned?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Reset any supplied URL silently\n- **B.** Require an interactive prompt\n- **C.** Use an explicit documented reset now and add guarded targets later\n\n**Chosen: C — Use an explicit documented reset now and add guarded targets later**\n\ndb:reset drops and recreates the configured public schema, migrates and seeds it. It currently has no database-name allowlist. Guarded reset targets and a dedicated test database are intended improvements; the shared workflow is for disposable demo data.\n\n### Q52 — Which local ports and database setup should be used?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Common ports and a separate test database\n- **B.** Random ports on every run\n- **C.** Stable overridable ports and one configured demo database\n\n**Chosen: C — Stable overridable ports and one configured demo database**\n\nVite, Express and PostgreSQL default to ports 47831, 47832 and 47833, with environment overrides. To save setup and maintenance time in this local exercise, tests share the configured application database. A dedicated test database is the intended improvement. Do not run the app and database-backed suites concurrently or use this workflow with real production data.\n\n## API and security boundaries\n\n### Q53 — How should the browser reach Express during development?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Permissive CORS\n- **B.** Direct cross-origin API calls\n- **C.** Vite's same-origin /api proxy\n\n**Chosen: C — Vite's same-origin /api proxy**\n\nVite proxies same-origin /api requests to Express. The application does not enable permissive CORS.\n\n### Q54 — How should CSRF be addressed?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Rely only on SameSite\n- **B.** Add a CSRF-token framework\n- **C.** Validate Origin alongside cookie protections\n\n**Chosen: C — Validate Origin alongside cookie protections**\n\nState-changing requests must carry the configured Origin, including sign-in. A missing or different Origin is rejected. SameSite=Lax cookies provide an additional browser control; there is no separate CSRF-token framework.\n\n### Q55 — Should endpoint names be obscured for security?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Obscure or random paths\n- **B.** Conventional descriptive routes\n- **C.** One generic RPC endpoint\n\n**Chosen: B — Conventional descriptive routes**\n\nEndpoints use descriptive paths. Session-based authorization, validation, rate limits and controlled responses provide the protection.\n\n### Q56 — How should SQL injection be prevented?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Manually sanitize strings\n- **B.** Concatenate escaped values\n- **C.** Use parameterized pg queries\n\n**Chosen: C — Use parameterized pg queries**\n\nRequest values use parameterized pg queries. Database creation separately quotes the configured database identifier; value parameters cannot stand in for SQL identifiers.\n\n### Q57 — How should request data be validated?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** TypeScript types only\n- **B.** Scattered manual checks\n- **C.** Strict runtime schemas plus database constraints\n\n**Chosen: C — Strict runtime schemas plus database constraints**\n\nStrict Zod schemas reject unexpected fields and malformed values at the HTTP boundary. JSON bodies are limited to 20 KB. The money module independently parses amounts, and database constraints protect stored state.\n\n### Q58 — How much should errors reveal?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Expose detailed internal reasons\n- **B.** Return one message for every failure\n- **C.** Use safe stable codes with deliberately limited detail\n\n**Chosen: C — Use safe stable codes with deliberately limited detail**\n\nExpected failures use stable codes and safe messages. Unknown emails and wrong passwords receive the same response; missing recipients and self-transfers share an unavailable response. Unexpected errors return a generic message and request ID rather than SQL or stack traces.\n\n### Q59 — Which requests should be rate-limited?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** No rate limiting\n- **B.** Limit sign-in only\n- **C.** Limit failed sign-ins and recipient/transfer operations\n\n**Chosen: C — Limit failed sign-ins and recipient/transfer operations**\n\nFailed sign-ins are limited to five per normalized email/IP per fifteen minutes. Preview and transfer share twenty requests per authenticated user per minute. Limiters are in memory for the local server; deposit and withdrawal have no dedicated limiter.\n\n### Q60 — What may application logs contain?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Full request bodies\n- **B.** Broad debug logging\n- **C.** Minimal metadata correlated by request ID\n\n**Chosen: C — Minimal metadata correlated by request ID**\n\nAccess logs record request ID, method, route, status and duration without request bodies. Unexpected errors are logged server-side with the request ID, while clients receive a generic response. Financial operation records live separately in money_transactions.\n\n## Frontend and testing\n\n### Q61 — How should frontend state be managed?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Redux\n- **B.** A server-state library\n- **C.** Session context plus local form state\n\n**Chosen: C — Session context plus local form state**\n\nReact context owns session state; forms use local state and transfer navigation state. Native fetch handles API requests. The app does not require Redux or a server-state library.\n\n### Q62 — What testing strategy demonstrates the important decisions?\n\n**Options considered**\n\n- **A.** Mocked unit tests only\n- **B.** Manual testing only\n- **C.** Real PostgreSQL integration tests plus focused client and browser tests\n\n**Chosen: C — Real PostgreSQL integration tests plus focused client and browser tests**\n\nReal PostgreSQL integration tests exercise authentication, authorization, atomicity, rollback, concurrency, idempotency and constraints. Client tests cover forms and retry behavior. Three Playwright journeys cover deposits/withdrawals, a successful transfer and declined transfers. Successful test commands reset demo data; failures retain state for diagnosis.\n";
